# Migrating Flat Pulumi Program to ComponentResource with Aliases

## Problem

When you wrap existing resources into a `ComponentResource`, Pulumi sees new URNs (the type changes from `aws:s3/bucket:Bucket` to `myapp:web:S3Bucket` etc.) and **deletes + recreates** everything. Fix: pass `aliases` on each child resource pointing at the old URNs.

## 1. Before: Flat program (`index.ts`)

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack();

export const bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2("assets", { /* ... */ });
export const db = new aws.rds.Instance("db", { /* ... */ });
export const cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster("cluster", { /* ... */ });
```

## 2. After: ComponentResource (`web-app.ts`)

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

export interface WebAppArgs {
    env: "dev" | "prod";
    region: string;
    dbPassword: pulumi.Output<string>;
}

export class WebApp extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
    public readonly bucket: aws.s3.BucketV2;
    public readonly db: aws.rds.Instance;
    public readonly cluster: aws.eks.Cluster;
    public readonly dbConnString: pulumi.Output<string>;

    constructor(name: string, args: WebAppArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
        super("myapp:web:WebApp", name, {}, opts);

        const bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2(`${name}-assets`, {
            tags: { env: args.env },
        }, { parent: this });

        const db = new aws.rds.Instance(`${name}-db`, {
            instanceClass: args.env === "prod" ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
            engine: "postgres",
            multiAz: args.env === "prod",
            storageEncrypted: true,
            publiclyAccessible: false,
            backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
            deletionProtection: args.env === "prod",
            password: args.dbPassword,
            username: "appuser",
        }, { parent: this });

        const cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(`${name}-cluster`, {
            /* ... */
        }, { parent: this });

        this.bucket = bucket;
        this.db = db;
        this.cluster = cluster;
        this.dbConnString = pulumi.interpolate`postgresql://appuser:${db.password}@${db.endpoint}/app`;

        this.registerOutputs();
    }
}
```

## 3. Migration with aliases — the critical part

Resource names inside the component get prefixed (`app-assets` vs old `assets`), and the type stays the same, so aliases must cover the **name change**:

```ts
// index.ts
const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const dbPassword = cfg.getSecret("dbPassword");

const app = new WebApp("app", {
    env: pulumi.getStack() as "dev" | "prod",
    region: aws.getRegionOutput().name,
    dbPassword: dbPassword!,
}, {
    aliases: [{ urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::flat-program::aws:s3/bucketV2:BucketV2::assets" },
              { urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::flat-program::aws:rds/instance:Instance::db" },
              { urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::flat-program::aws:eks:Cluster::cluster" }],
});
```

**Preferred, cleaner approach** — alias by name inside the component, so the alias travels with the resource:

```ts
// web-app.ts
const bucket = new aws.s3.BucketV2(`${name}-assets`, { /* ... */ }, {
    parent: this,
    aliases: ["assets"],                    // old flat-program name
});

const db = new aws.rds.Instance(`${name}-db`, { /* ... */ }, {
    parent: this,
    aliases: ["db"],
});

const cluster = new aws.eks.Cluster(`${name}-cluster`, { /* ... */ }, {
    parent: this,
    aliases: ["cluster"],
});
```

Aliases also accept `pulumi.output(...)` URNs and type transforms for bulk moves.

## 4. Migration procedure

```bash
pulumi stack select dev
pulumi preview          # should show "changes" but ~outputs / no `replace` or `±replaced`
pulumi up               # state-only URN migration, zero infra changes
pulumi stack select prod && pulumi up   # same aliases work — stack name is part of URN per stack
```

## Key rules

1. **Aliases live on the child resource** (or inherited via `aliases` on the component opts, which propagate to children — useful for bulk migration).
2. Alias must match the **old URN's name and type** exactly (or the full URN).
3. **Don't change any properties in the same `pulumi up`** as the alias migration — split into two updates to verify no replacement.
4. Once applied, remove aliases in a later commit (Pulumi keeps them in state history; safe to drop after a successful run).
5. Component-level `aliases: [{ parent: "old-parent" }]` (a.k.a. legacy parent aliasing) can handle cases where the resource was previously under a different parent.

## Result

`pulumi preview` shows no `±replaced` resources — only URN remapping in state. Dev and prod migrate independently with the same code.
